 "title": "St Petersburg, 6 April 1783 (O.S.): the eight cipher lines",
 "caption": "The whole passage, 215 numbers as the clerk’s copy has them (the triplicate lacks seven at the frame edge), each with the letter Dana’s table gives for the column in use. 29 is not in the table. Three numbers that Dana put in the wrong column show the table’s own letter (r, g and s where the sense wants i, y and e) and are marked as doubtful. NARA, Papers of the Continental Congress, item 89 vol. 2 p. 730 and item 109 p. 202.",
 "unit": "numbers",
 "key_note": "Three alphabets of 27 signs (a to z and &) used in turn: the columns W, a, r of Dana’s sheet. The small letter after a number is the column it is read in. A number above 27 is a word from his list and starts the key word again. One column is passed over after the b of irresistab.",
